fact WorkItem(id: "WI-510", description: some(value: "Diagnostics: the TypeError -> LoadError remapping (TypeError::to_load_error, kb/typing.rs ~351 for TypeMismatch and ~487 for Other) is LOSSY and hinders debugging. It flattens the structured TypeErrorContext (EntityField{entity,field} / OperationArgument{op,param} / LetBinding / ... ) and the Value-typed expected/actual into plain display STRINGS (LoadError::TypeMismatch{entity_name:String, field_name:String, expected_type:String, actual_type:String}), and DROPS the variant identity and the CONSTRUCTION SITE. Consequence (hit hard in WI-509): two structurally-different errors render identically (e.g. an EntityField field-arg check and an OperationArgument arg check both surface as 'entity_name=field_access, field_name=field'), and a backtrace captured AT THE CONVERSION reveals nothing because the TypeError is built, pushed into an errors Vec, and remapped later in bulk (type_check_sorts ~18799) -- so you must instrument at CONSTRUCTION, not conversion, to find where an error originates. INVESTIGATE/OPTIONS: (a) carry the originating source location / a lightweight construction marker (or a captured backtrace under a debug cfg/env) on TypeError so the conversion can attribute it; (b) keep LoadError structured (preserve the TypeErrorContext variant + the Value expected/actual) rather than pre-stringifying, stringify only at final render; (c) at minimum, include the TypeError variant name + a distinguishing tag in the rendered message so identical-looking errors are disambiguable. ACCEPTANCE: a field_access (or any) TypeMismatch can be traced to its construction site without hand-instrumenting each candidate; the rendered diagnostic distinguishes EntityField vs OperationArgument origins; full anthill-core green. REF: WI-509 (debugging cost); kb/typing.rs to_load_error + TypeErrorContext::entity_name/field_name; LoadError::TypeMismatch. Rust-only (diagnostics, no semantic change)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T11:04:25Z"))
